Let me think about the cleanest Lean proof of the sharp direct bound. There are two sub-approaches:

Approach A (integral representation): For K > 0, let a = √K. Then:
- j_K(t) = sin(a t)/a, j_K'(t) = cos(a t).
- t·j_K'(t) - j_K(t) = t cos(at) - sin(at)/a = -(1/a)(sin(at) - at cos(at)).
- Claim: |sin x - x cos x| ≤ x³/3 for x ≥ 0. Proof: define f x = x³/3 - (sin x - x cos x); f 0 = 0; f' x = x² - x sin x = x(x - sin x) ≥ 0 for x ≥ 0 (since sin x ≤ x for x ≥ 0). Then f ≥ 0. Also sin x - x cos x ≥ 0 for x ∈ [0,π] via g x = sin x - x cos x, g 0 = 0, g' x = x sin x ≥ 0.
- j_K(t) ≥ t - K t³/6? For the sharp version need sin x ≥ x - x³/6, standard. Alternatively we can get away with a weaker lower bound but then C would be less clean.

Then:
|j'/j - 1/t| = |t j' - j|/(t j) ≤ (x³/(3a))/(t·j). And t·j = t sin x/a = x sin x/a². So ratio = (x³/(3a))·(a²/(x sin x)) = a x²/(3 sin x) = √K (K t²)/(3 sin x)... Let me recompute: a x²/(3 sin x) with x = at: = a·a²t²/(3 sin(at)) = a³ t²/(3 sin(at)) = K·a t²/(3 sin) = K t·(at)/(3 sin(at)) = K t · x/(3 sin x).
So |j'/j - 1/t| ≤ K t · x/(3 sin x), and sin x ≥ x - x³/6 = x(1 - x²/6) gives x/(3 sin x) ≤ 1/(3(1-x²/6)). For x² ≤ 3/2... say x ≤ 1: ≤ 1/(3·(5/6)) = 2/5. So ≤ (2/5) K t ≤ K t.

So the theorem: for K ≥ 0, t > 0, K t² ≤ 1 (i.e. x ≤ 1): |j_K'(t)/j_K(t) - 1/t| ≤ K t. Also need j_K(t) ≠ 0.

Careful at K = 0: j = t, j' = 1, so difference = 0 ≤ 0 = K t. Fine.

